-- Conformance: `resume` is not available as a function name (Chapter 5, Section 5.2)
-- Tests: E153 — a user fn named `resume` is rejected at its declaration.
-- Unlike the other reserved names this one is no parse-level trap: `resume` is
-- not a keyword token in `vera/grammar.lark`, so the declaration parses and a
-- bare `resume(7)` outside a handler resolves to it and runs.  What it collides
-- with is the binding the checker injects into every handler clause body for
-- the effect-resumption operator, which would give one spelling two meanings
-- by position.  Chapter 1, Section 1.4 reserves the identifier; this gate
-- enforces it, and alone: a clause body in the same file still resolves
-- `resume` to the operator, so a rejected declaration draws no second error.
public fn resume(@Int -> @Int)
  requires(true)
  ensures(@Int.result >= 0)
  effects(pure)
{
  5
}

public fn main(@Unit -> @Int)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  0
}
